            <ArticleTitle>Cross-cultural adaptation of a German version of the Oswestry Disability Index and evaluation of its measurement properties.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E448-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Psychometric testing of a translated, culturally adapted questionnaire.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Cross-cultural adaptation of a German version of the Oswestry Disability Index (ODI) and evaluation of its measurement properties.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The ODI, one of the most popular questionnaires for chronic low back pain (LBP), has been valid, reliable, and responsive. Recently, a Swiss version of the ODI has been published, but there is no validated version for Germany to date.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The translated and adapted German version of the ODI (ODI-G) was validated in inpatients with chronic LBP during 3 weeks' medical rehabilitation care. The ODI-G was completed at admission, 1 day later, and at discharge. Comparison with both a generic and chronic LBP-specific measure (the SF-36 and Hannover Functional Ability Questionnaire) assessed criterion validity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A very high level of test-retest-reliability was found (r = 0.91). Criterion validity showed high correlations between the ODI-G on 1 side, and the SF-36 and Hannover Functional Ability Questionnaire on the other. Standardized response means showed significant changes when health status improved (1.38) or deteriorated (1.35).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The ODI-G is valid, reliable, and responsive. It may be used to measure current state as well as changes in health status, and allows for cross-cultural comparisons. Further research comparing the 2 versions in German language seems to be necessary.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
